Chapter: CapDirection

 Direction is the only attenuation axis the capability model has, and
 until now the compiler threw it away: manifest-cap-bytes wrote a
 literal readwrite for every capability it emitted, and boot-cap-mask-for
 did not look at direction at all. So a program declaring
 [FileSystem.Read] was granted filesystem READ AND WRITE at boot, and put
 a READWRITE capability into its own manifest for any verifier that read
 it. The loader's dir-bits honoured read-only grants correctly the whole
 time -- it was simply being told the wrong thing.

 This opening declares FileSystem.Read and nothing else of that family.
 It reads its own capability word back out of the process table and
 reports the two filesystem bits: read is bit 6, write is bit 7.

   read=1 write=0   the row was honoured. The only passing answer.
   read=1 write=1   direction was ignored and the program was handed an
                    authority it never asked for. That is what this test
                    exists to catch, and it is what the machine did
                    before today.
   read=0           the grant was lost entirely; a program that declared
                    the effect cannot use it.

 Console is declared bare, so it asks for both directions and must get
 them -- an attenuation that also denies what was legitimately asked for
 is not attenuation, it is a bug. Reporting the console bits proves the
 derivation does not simply clear every write bit it sees.

 The bits are read through process-get-cap, which is what the kernel
 itself tests before it services a syscall. This is the granted word, not
 a restatement of the source.

Section: Bits

  cap-bit : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  cap-bit (word) (n) = if bit-and word (bit-shl 1 n) /= 0 then 1 else 0

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console, FileSystem.Read] Nothing
  opening = act
    print-line-uni ("fs-read "  & show (cap-bit (process-get-cap process-get-pid) 6))
    print-line-uni ("fs-write " & show (cap-bit (process-get-cap process-get-pid) 7))
    print-line-uni ("con-read "  & show (cap-bit (process-get-cap process-get-pid) 4))
    print-line-uni ("con-write " & show (cap-bit (process-get-cap process-get-pid) 5))
  end
